# Installation

{{ display_name }} publishes the same skills to four runtimes: Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and Pi. The skills are generated into `skills/` and the plugin manifests at the repository root.

Your agent can do the install. Copy this prompt into your coding agent:

```text
Install the {{ repo_name }} skills: pick the best installation method for
you from {{ repo_url }}/blob/main/docs/installation.md
```

The prompt points at this page, not at a command, so the routes below can change without the prompt changing.

## For agents reading this page

You were pointed here to install the skills. Do this:

1. Pick the route in the table below that you can run yourself on your platform. Prefer a route native to your runtime over a manual clone.
2. If a route needs a slash command or an interactive step you cannot run, give the user the exact command and ask them to run it. Do not guess at another command.
3. Confirm the install with the route's confirm step.
4. Plugin and skills installs usually load in a new session, not in the conversation that installed them. If the skills are not available to you now, read `skills/<name>/SKILL.md` from a clone of this repository (a temporary clone is fine) and tell the user to start a new session so the installed skills load next time.
5. If a route fails, fall back to the manual route. Do not report the install as done until you have confirmed the skills or read a `SKILL.md`.
6. Do not install other agent skills or switch to a different route unless this one fails.

## Routes

| Runtime | Install | Update | Confirm |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Claude Code | `claude plugin marketplace add {{ github_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}`, then `claude plugin install {{ repo_name }}@{{ repo_name }}` (in a session: `/plugin marketplace add {{ github_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}` and `/plugin install {{ repo_name }}@{{ repo_name }}`) | `claude plugin marketplace update {{ repo_name }}`, then `claude plugin update {{ repo_name }}@{{ repo_name }}` | `claude plugin list` shows the plugin and its version; run `/reload-plugins` or start a new session to load it |
| Codex | clone this repository, then `codex plugin marketplace add /absolute/path/to/{{ repo_name }}` and `codex plugin add {{ repo_name }}@{{ repo_name }}`; start a new thread | pull the clone, then `codex plugin add {{ repo_name }}@{{ repo_name }}` again, and start a new thread | the plugin appears in Codex's plugin list in a new thread |
| OpenCode | git only; see [OpenCode](#opencode) | `git pull` in the clone | the skills are offered by OpenCode's `skill` tool in a new session |
{%- if publish_to_npm %}
| Pi | `pi install npm:{{ repo_name }}` or `pi install git:github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}` | `pi update`, then restart Pi or reload resources | invoke one of the skills with `/skill:<name>` |
{%- else %}
| Pi | `pi install git:github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}` (not published to npm) | `pi update`, then restart Pi or reload resources | invoke one of the skills with `/skill:<name>` |
{%- endif %}
| Manual | clone this repository and read `skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, or copy `skills/*` into your agent's skills directory | `git pull` in the clone, then copy `skills/*` again if you copied them | the clone's `git rev-parse HEAD` matches the latest commit on `main` |

The Pi package declares the `pi-package` keyword and a `pi.skills` entry pointing at `./skills` in `package.json`. To compare an installed version with the latest, use the version in `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` or the repository's latest GitHub release.

## OpenCode

OpenCode installs from git only. Clone the repository, then make each skill discoverable. OpenCode searches `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` in the project and `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` globally, so link every folder under `skills/` into one of those:

```sh
git clone {{ repo_url }}
mkdir -p ~/.agents/skills
for skill in {{ repo_name }}/skills/*/; do ln -s "$PWD/${skill%/}" ~/.agents/skills/; done
```

Alternatively, add the clone's `skills/` directory to OpenCode's configuration:

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "skills": ["/absolute/path/to/{{ repo_name }}/skills"]
}
```

To update, run `git pull` in the clone; nothing was copied.

> **Open question.** The official OpenCode documentation shows no way for an npm package to ship skills, so there is no npm route for OpenCode. If that changes, add it here.
